.TH ENCOPT-TOOLS 1 "2026-11-10" "encopt 1.1" "pdp64 tools"
.SH NAME
export.py, profile.py, leave-one-out.py \- the exporter, the profile collector and the overfitting check of encopt(1)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B export.py
.BI \-\-corpus " dir"
[\fB\-\-out\fR \fIfile\fR] [\fB\-\-weights\fR \fIfile\fR]... [\fB\-\-budget\fR \fIfraction\fR] [\fB\-\-lam\fR \fIbytes\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-cb3\fR] [\fB\-\-prev\fR \fIresult\fR]
.br
.B export.py \-\-corpus
.I dir
.BI \-\-verify " result"
.br
.B profile.py \-o
.I weights
[\fB\-\-tests\fR \fIdir\fR]... [\fB\-\-program\fR \fIname\fR=\fIfile\fR,... [\fB\-\-cppflags\fR \fIname\fR=\fIflags\fR]]... [\fB\-\-keep\fR \fIdir\fR]
.br
.B leave-one-out.py \-\-corpus
.I dir
.B \-\-weights\-dir
.I dir
[\fB\-\-objective\fR \fIobjective\fR] [\fB\-\-budget\fR \fIfraction\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B export.py
writes the input file of
.BR encopt (1)
(format in
.BR encopt (5))
from the compiled assembly of the corpus (\fIdir\fR: one \fI.s\fR per source file, \fBccom\fR over the preprocessed corpus of tools/arch-compare), using the operand model and the
candidate forms of the Python study in docs/codegen-data/encoding.  It reads the corpus with the corrected reader
.I corpusfix.py
(see CORPUS READER).
.TP
.BI \-\-budget " fraction"
The share of the 65,536 first-parcel patterns that the forced and the chosen forms may use.  The default is 1.0: \fIno reserve\fR, the free-space policy is zero, and the candidates may use all that
the forced forms leave (65,536 minus the forced forms).  The study kept 0.95 (62,259 patterns).
.TP
.BI \-\-weights " file"
A weights file (\fBencopt\fR(5), WEIGHTS FILE), repeatable.  Its dynamic counts become the dynamic weights of the demands, which the objectives \fBfetch\fR and \fBexec\fR of encopt need.
.TP
.BI \-\-lam " bytes"
Bytes charged for the extra instruction of a compare followed by a branch where no compare-and-branch form reaches (default 2).
.TP
.BI \-\-prev " result"
Take the distances of the corpus from the layout of the selection in an earlier result (the next round of the design loop).
.TP
.BI \-\-verify " result"
Size the corpus with the forms chosen in \fIresult\fR with the real layout, branch relaxation and a round trip of every instruction.
.SH CORPUS READER
The study's reader (m2.py) takes a word that is neither register, number nor memory form for a label.  \fBset cc,rd\fR (a condition code and a register) and the system-register moves
\fBmfsr rd,sreg\fR and \fBmtsr sreg,rs\fR take a word of their own (spec/pdp64-asm.hbnf: \fIcondinsn\fR, \fIsrinsn\fR), so they were read as having a label operand and no form fitted
them: 337 to 342 instructions of the corpus counted as unencodable in every figure.  \fIcorpusfix.py\fR wraps the reader: the word becomes its number (the \fBcc\fR and \fBsysreg\fR tables of
spec/pdp64-mnemonics.hbnf) as an immediate.  The condition codes of \fBtrapcc\fR and \fBcb\fR are in the mnemonic, a \fBcb\fR has a label as its last operand and no condition word: nothing else
takes a word.  The history scripts are not edited.  tests/test_reader.py is the test.
.SH PROFILE COLLECTOR
.B profile.py
compiles, links and runs programs under \fBsim64 \-P\fR and writes the dynamic count of each distinct instruction as a weights file.  \fB\-\-tests\fR takes directories of the
test programs of tools/pdp64test.sh (a failing test is left out); \fB\-\-program\fR a program of its own files (C, or preprocessed \fI.i\fR) with its own \fBmain\fR, linked with the start code
of the tests;
\fB\-\-cppflags\fR gives the preprocessor flags of the C files of one program.  The decoding is \fBencdis \-k\fR (tools/encdis.c), which reads the existing \fB\-P\fR profile and
the executable; sim64 is not changed.  The counts add up to the instructions that sim64 reports (checked).
.I programs/make-profile.sh
runs the five programs of the repository's profile (lz4, zlib and three BearSSL programs; the main of each and a small stand-in C library are in programs/).
.SH OVERFITTING
.B leave-one-out.py
trains the optimum on all profiled programs but one and measures that one, under the committed grammar, under the optimum trained on all (seen) and under the optimum trained
on the others (unseen), one line per program.  It needs one weights file per program (\fIname\fB.weights\fR in the directory).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR encopt (1),
.BR encopt (5),
.I docs/encoding-work/optimizer.md
